WITH expected AS (SELECT table_name,row_key,data FROM jsonb_to_recordset('[{"table_name":"edition","row_key":{"code":"campus"},"data":{"code":"campus","name":"校园版","sort_order":2}},{"table_name":"book","row_key":{"edition_code":"campus","book_no":3},"data":{"edition_code":"campus","book_no":3,"title":"校园版 3","sort_order":3}},{"table_name":"exam_paper","row_key":{"slug":"campus-3-test-1"},"data":{"edition_code":"campus","book_no":3,"test_no":1,"slug":"campus-3-test-1","title":"FCE青少版真题3 Test 1 · 阅读与语用讲评","subtitle":"Reading and Use of English（1小时15分钟）· Part 1–Part 7","duration_minutes":75,"status":"published","edition_note":"更新版 · 仅阅读与语用","source_metadata":{"exam_page":2,"publication_status":"用户提供的FCE青少版真题3 Test 1扫描原卷；阅读与语用为PDF第2–13页。","links":[],"paper_file":"题目/FCE题目/校园版3/青3-T1.pdf","answer_file":"题目/FCE题目/FCE-ANSWERS/校园版/F03-T1.pdf","pdf_pages":[2,13],"printed_pages":[8,19],"supplement_file":"FCE网站最终版/campus-3-test-1/index.html"}}},{"table_name":"exam_section","row_key":{"code":"P1"},"data":{"code":"P1","part_no":1,"title":"Part 1","question_format":"multiple_choice_cloze","paper_reference":"青3-T1.pdf 第2–3页","expected_option_count":4,"sort_order":1,"source_groups":[]}},{"table_name":"exam_section","row_key":{"code":"P2"},"data":{"code":"P2","part_no":2,"title":"Part 2","question_format":"open_cloze","paper_reference":"青3-T1.pdf 第4页","expected_option_count":0,"sort_order":2,"source_groups":[]}},{"table_name":"exam_section","row_key":{"code":"P3"},"data":{"code":"P3","part_no":3,"title":"Part 3","question_format":"word_formation","paper_reference":"青3-T1.pdf 第5页","expected_option_count":0,"sort_order":3,"source_groups":[]}},{"table_name":"exam_section","row_key":{"code":"P4"},"data":{"code":"P4","part_no":4,"title":"Part 4","question_format":"key_word_transformation","paper_reference":"青3-T1.pdf 第6–7页","expected_option_count":0,"sort_order":4,"source_groups":[]}},{"table_name":"exam_section","row_key":{"code":"P5"},"data":{"code":"P5","part_no":5,"title":"Part 5","question_format":"reading_multiple_choice","paper_reference":"青3-T1.pdf 第8–9页","expected_option_count":4,"sort_order":5,"source_groups":[]}},{"table_name":"exam_section","row_key":{"code":"P6"},"data":{"code":"P6","part_no":6,"title":"Part 6","question_format":"gapped_text","paper_reference":"青3-T1.pdf 第10–11页","expected_option_count":7,"sort_order":6,"source_groups":[]}},{"table_name":"exam_section","row_key":{"code":"P7"},"data":{"code":"P7","part_no":7,"title":"Part 7","question_format":"multiple_matching","paper_reference":"青3-T1.pdf 第12–13页","expected_option_count":5,"sort_order":7,"source_groups":[{"title":"A","paragraph_ids":["P7-h","P7-Ah","P7-A"]},{"title":"B","paragraph_ids":["P7-Bh","P7-B"]},{"title":"C","paragraph_ids":["P7-Ch","P7-C"]},{"title":"D","paragraph_ids":["P7-Dh","P7-D"]},{"title":"E","paragraph_ids":["P7-Eh","P7-E"]}]}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P1","source_key":"P1-h"},"data":{"section_code":"P1","source_key":"P1-h","role":"heading","text":"Chocolate teapots really are useful","translation":"巧克力茶壶真的有用","sort_order":1}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P1","source_key":"P1-p1"},"data":{"section_code":"P1","source_key":"P1-p1","role":"paragraph","text":"A chocolate teapot is not as useless as the old British saying (0) suggests. Scientists have shown it''s possible to use one to make tea. They''ve created a chocolate teapot that can be filled with boiling water and left to {{1}} for two minutes, after which the tea is ready to be served.","translation":"巧克力茶壶并不像英国那句老话所暗示的那样毫无用处。科学家已经证明，用它来泡茶是可能的。他们制成了一个可以注入沸水并静置{{1}}两分钟的巧克力茶壶，两分钟之后茶就可以倒出来享用了。","sort_order":2}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P1","source_key":"P1-p2"},"data":{"section_code":"P1","source_key":"P1-p2","role":"paragraph","text":"After {{2}} a range of experiments, the scientists {{3}} to the conclusion that the {{4}} to making a teapot that wouldn''t melt was to use dark chocolate with 65% chocolate solids and build up a series of layers to strengthen it. This whole process took around two-and-a-half hours.","translation":"在{{2}}一系列实验之后，科学家们{{3}}结论：做出一个不会融化的茶壶的{{4}}在于使用含65%可可固形物的黑巧克力，并一层层堆叠加固。整个过程花了大约两个半小时。","sort_order":3}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P1","source_key":"P1-p3"},"data":{"section_code":"P1","source_key":"P1-p3","role":"paragraph","text":"At first the scientists wondered whether they might {{5}} end up with chocolate tea. They discovered some of the chocolate on the inside of the pot would melt, but {{6}} that the water was poured in carefully, the {{7}} majority of it wouldn''t be affected. When they tried the tea, the team agreed it was lovely with a {{8}} taste of chocolate.","translation":"起初，科学家们担心他们到头来{{5}}只会得到一杯巧克力味的茶。他们发现壶内壁的一些巧克力会融化，但{{6}}只要小心注水，{{7}}绝大部分巧克力并不会受到影响。品尝时，团队一致认为这茶带着{{8}}淡淡的巧克力味，非常好喝。","sort_order":4}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P2","source_key":"P2-h"},"data":{"section_code":"P2","source_key":"P2-h","role":"heading","text":"Making mistakes helps you to succeed","translation":"犯错帮助你成功","sort_order":1}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P2","source_key":"P2-p1"},"data":{"section_code":"P2","source_key":"P2-p1","role":"paragraph","text":"(0) Have you ever tried a new sport or learnt to play a musical instrument? {{9}} so, you''ll know that once you figure {{10}} how to do it and get good at it, you won''t lose your skills, even when you haven''t practised for a long time. Most experts put this down to ''muscle memory'', which means the brain remembers an action and can recall it when needed. Now some researchers believe there''s another important factor: errors that occur while learning a task.","translation":"（例0）你是否尝试过一项新运动，或学过一种乐器？{{9}}如果是的话，你就会知道：一旦你{{10}}弄明白怎么做并且练得熟练了，即使很久不练，你也不会丢掉这项技能。大多数专家把这归因于''肌肉记忆''——大脑记住了一个动作，需要时就能调用它。而如今一些研究人员认为还有一个重要因素：学习任务过程中出现的错误。","sort_order":2}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P2","source_key":"P2-p2"},"data":{"section_code":"P2","source_key":"P2-p2","role":"paragraph","text":"{{11}} surprised the researchers is that getting things wrong not only trains the brain to perform better at a specific task, but also helps it to learn faster. This is true even when mistakes are {{12}} while learning a completely new task. The researchers believe that the brain keeps a record {{13}} errors and draws on them whenever a new skill {{14}} being learnt. This ensures that mistakes aren''t repeated, and probably explains why people {{15}} master one sport or instrument can pick {{16}} others with relative ease.","translation":"{{11}}让研究人员感到惊讶的是，出错不仅能训练大脑在特定任务上表现得更好，还能帮助它学得更快。即使在学习一个全新任务时{{12}}犯了错，这一点同样成立。研究人员认为，大脑会{{13}}记录错误，并且每当学习一项新技能{{14}}时都会调用这些记录。这保证了错误不会重复出现，或许也解释了为什么{{15}}精通一项运动或乐器的人能相对轻松地{{16}}学会其他运动或乐器。","sort_order":3}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P3","source_key":"P3-h"},"data":{"section_code":"P3","source_key":"P3-h","role":"heading","text":"Have a go at kayaking","translation":"试试皮划艇吧","sort_order":1}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P3","source_key":"P3-p1"},"data":{"section_code":"P3","source_key":"P3-p1","role":"paragraph","text":"I''ve discovered an (0) amazing sport: kayaking. It looks very {{17}} when you see it on TV, and apparently it''s been increasing in {{18}} over the past few years. I''m not actually a very sporty person but when my sister, a keen kayaker herself, bought me a lesson for my birthday that was my opportunity to have a go.","translation":"我发现了一项（例0）了不起的运动：皮划艇。在电视上看到它时，它看起来非常{{17}}，而且显然这几年它越来越{{18}}了。其实我并不是个特别爱运动的人，但当我那本身就热衷皮划艇的姐姐送我一节课作为生日礼物时，那就成了我尝试一次的机会。","sort_order":2}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P3","source_key":"P3-p2"},"data":{"section_code":"P3","source_key":"P3-p2","role":"paragraph","text":"It was a {{19}} to discover I wasn''t the only beginner – everyone else was also very {{20}} like me. At first we were all pretty {{21}}, and some of us even fell in the water, but we learnt quickly and our confidence began to grow.","translation":"发现我不是唯一的初学者真是让人{{19}}——其他人和我一样都非常{{20}}。起初我们都相当{{21}}，有的人甚至还掉进了水里，但我们学得很快，信心也开始增长。","sort_order":3}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P3","source_key":"P3-p3"},"data":{"section_code":"P3","source_key":"P3-p3","role":"paragraph","text":"I loved being on the river. The {{22}} were so beautiful and relaxing that I was really reluctant to get out of the kayak when the lesson finished! It was a truly {{23}} day. I signed up for a one-week course without a moment''s hesitation. Now I''m just as {{24}} about kayaking as my sister.","translation":"我喜欢待在河上。四周的{{22}}如此美丽、令人放松，以至于课程结束时我真的很不情愿从皮划艇里出来！那真是{{23}}的一天。我毫不犹豫地报名了一个为期一周的课程。现在我对皮划艇和姐姐一样{{24}}了。","sort_order":4}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P4","source_key":"P4-p0"},"data":{"section_code":"P4","source_key":"P4-p0","role":"caption","text":"For questions 25-30, complete the second sentence so that it has a similar meaning to the first sentence, using the word given. Do not change the word given. You must use between two and five words, including the word given.","translation":"第25-30题：用所给单词完成第二个句子，使其与第一个句子意思相近。不得改变所给单词的形式。所填部分（含所给单词在内）必须为两到五个单词。","sort_order":1}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P5","source_key":"P5-h"},"data":{"section_code":"P5","source_key":"P5-h","role":"heading","text":"Young writer","translation":"少年作家","sort_order":1}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P5","source_key":"P5-p1"},"data":{"section_code":"P5","source_key":"P5-p1","role":"paragraph","text":"Charlotte Grainger explains that it was her primary school teacher who first speculated that she might write a novel. ''I thought the height of achievement would be to write a book because it seemed such a challenge and anyway I didn''t know what other teenagers were doing, like being in the Olympics, for example. When I turned 13 I thought: \"I may as well attempt this now\".'' The novel Charlotte started then is, remarkably, about to be published. ''Recently I told my ex-teacher about it and she was astonished. She told me she''d meant I''d probably do it when I was 30 or 40. That had never occurred to me – I couldn''t understand why I''d be expected to put something on hold that I had a chance of being good at.''","translation":"夏洛特·格雷杰解释说，最先猜测她可能写小说的，是她的小学老师。''我当时认为，人生成就的顶点就是写一本书，因为那看起来极具挑战；而且我也不知道别的青少年都在做什么，比如参加奥运会之类的。13岁那年我心想：\"我不妨现在就试试。\"''夏洛特当时动笔的那部小说，令人惊讶地即将出版。''最近我告诉了以前的那位老师，她非常吃惊。她说她的意思是我大概要到三四十岁才会去写。我从来没这么想过——我不明白，为什么明明有把握做好的事，却要被要求搁置。''","sort_order":2}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P5","source_key":"P5-p2"},"data":{"section_code":"P5","source_key":"P5-p2","role":"paragraph","text":"It''s a winter afternoon, in the offices of Charlotte''s publishers. Even though I knew that I was going to interview a 15-year-old girl about her book, I was still momentarily thrown by seeing a teenager sitting there. The book is a huge achievement: the narrative is assured and action packed. Nothing about it, except the age of its heroine, suggests that it was written by a teenager. The public relations representative for the book is keeping us company in case Charlotte might need defending. But she needs neither parental nor professional support. She''s her own person: spirited, with an alert face and a lively intelligence, but also a steadiness that prevents any overconfidence she could be forgiven for feeling.","translation":"那是一个冬日的下午，地点在夏洛特出版社的办公室。尽管我早知道要采访一位15岁的少女、聊她的书，但看到一个少年坐在那里，我仍有一瞬间的错愕。这本书是一项巨大的成就：叙事沉稳自信、情节紧凑。除了女主角的年龄，这本书没有任何地方显示出它出自少年之手。出版社的公关代表全程陪同，以防夏洛特需要''被保护''。但她既不需要父母撑腰，也不需要专业人士护航。她完全是个有主见的人：朝气蓬勃，神情机敏，头脑灵活，同时又有一份沉稳，压住了任何她''情有可原''的自负。","sort_order":3}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P5","source_key":"P5-p3"},"data":{"section_code":"P5","source_key":"P5-p3","role":"paragraph","text":"I ask her about the sophisticated moral issues she raises. ''I was trying to ask big questions, not answer them. There aren''t many life lessons I can pass on to my readers,'' she responds, laughing. ''I don''t think my understanding of the world is limited by my age, but neither do I believe I''m particularly wise. I was just interested in exploring the theme of taking responsibility.''","translation":"我问她书中探讨的那些复杂的道德问题。''我是想提出大问题，而不是回答它们。能传递给读者的人生道理并不多，''她笑着回答。''我不认为我对世界的理解受年龄限制，但我也不觉得自己特别睿智。我只是对探索''承担责任''这个主题感兴趣。''","sort_order":4}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P5","source_key":"P5-p4"},"data":{"section_code":"P5","source_key":"P5-p4","role":"paragraph","text":"She has a theory about teenagers and the way they are ''betrayed'' by the fiction that is specifically aimed at them. There are, she maintains, three types of teenager depicted in novels. ''There''s the outsider who becomes acceptable to society, the naive teenager who knows nothing about the big wide world, and the awkward teenager character who isn''t socially skilled.'' Repeatedly reading about these character types irritated her. ''What if you''re basically fine? There aren''t many stories out there about characters who aren''t inadequate in some way. The overall impression teenagers can get from some writers – and I don''t think it''s intentional – is teenagers can''t possibly know who they are because they''re not experienced enough to know the truth. And when that is being pushed on them by writers, it can undermine their self-belief.''","translation":"她对青少年如何被''专门写给他们的小说''''辜负''有一套自己的理论。她认为，小说里的青少年有三类：''融入社会而变得合群的局外人；对广阔世界一无所知的天真少年；以及不善社交的笨拙少年。''反复读到这些人物类型让她很恼火。''可如果你本来好好的呢？市面上写''各方面都不欠缺''的人物的故事并不多。青少年从某些作家那里得到的整体印象（我认为作家并非有意如此）是：青少年不可能知道自己是谁，因为他们的阅历不足以认识真相。而当作家们把这种想法不断灌输给他们时，就会削弱他们的自信。''","sort_order":5}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P5","source_key":"P5-p5"},"data":{"section_code":"P5","source_key":"P5-p5","role":"paragraph","text":"Charlotte has always been a keen reader of famous fantasy writers, some of whom you might suppose she''d be indebted to, but in fact they almost put her off writing entirely. ''Books by my favourite fantasy writers explore deep things about psychology and about life. I was asking myself: is this seriously what I have to be doing to write a good book and am I really up to it?'' She does, however, praise the influence of a book called How Not to Write a Novel. ''It tells you that if the reader starts to guess what''s going to happen, the suspense has probably gone.''","translation":"夏洛特一直是著名奇幻作家们的忠实读者——你可能以为她从他们那里受益匪浅，但实际上，这些作家几乎让她彻底放弃了写作。''我最喜欢的奇幻作家们的书探索的是心理与人生这些深刻的东西。我当时问自己：要写出一本好书，真的得做到这种程度吗？我真的够格吗？''不过，有一本名为《如何不要写小说》的书，她确实称赞它影响了自己。''它告诉你：一旦读者开始猜到接下来会发生什么，悬念多半就没了。''","sort_order":6}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P5","source_key":"P5-p6"},"data":{"section_code":"P5","source_key":"P5-p6","role":"paragraph","text":"It would be easy to argue that Charlotte''s parents both being journalists was the decisive factor in getting her novel published. Indeed, her father''s acquaintance with someone in publishing was what got her book read initially. She acknowledges her good fortune, because getting anyone to read a first novel is hard, whatever the age of the author. ''Every aspiring writer needs support and a lucky break, but after that you''re on your own. Ultimately writers sink or swim according to their talents so I don''t take things for granted,'' Charlotte concludes.","translation":"人们很容易认为，夏洛特父母都是记者，这是她的小说得以出版的决定性因素。的确，父亲在出版界的人脉最初让她的书得到了被阅读的机会。她承认这份幸运，因为无论作者多大年纪，让一个''新人''愿意读你的第一部小说都很难。''每个怀抱雄心的作家都需要支持和一次运气，但那之后，路就要靠自己走了。说到底，作家凭才华决定沉浮，所以我不把任何事视作理所当然，''夏洛特总结道。","sort_order":7}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P6","source_key":"P6-h"},"data":{"section_code":"P6","source_key":"P6-h","role":"heading","text":"Lighting up the winter darkness","translation":"点亮冬日的黑暗","sort_order":1}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P6","source_key":"P6-p1"},"data":{"section_code":"P6","source_key":"P6-p1","role":"paragraph","text":"In northern Scandinavia the long dark winters can be difficult. The students in Dragonskolan, a secondary school in Umeå, in Sweden''s far north, are taking part in an experiment to see whether using intense electric light, known as ''full-spectrum'' light, can help with this problem. This light is much brighter than the lighting currently used in most schools and homes.","translation":"在斯堪的纳维亚北部，漫长黑暗的冬天会很不好过。瑞典最北部城市于默奥的中学 Dragonskolan 的学生们正在参与一项实验，检验使用被称为''全光谱''光的强电灯光是否能缓解这一问题。这种光比目前大多数学校和家里使用的照明要亮得多。","sort_order":2}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P6","source_key":"P6-p2"},"data":{"section_code":"P6","source_key":"P6-p2","role":"paragraph","text":"This experiment is also part of a bigger debate about using full-spectrum light in schools to relieve tiredness and lack of energy. These symptoms can develop when sleep patterns are disturbed by the very long nights. {{37}} It is not unusual, for example, for some students to fall asleep on the journey to school.","translation":"这项实验也是一场更大辩论的一部分：学校是否应使用全光谱光来缓解疲倦与精力不足。当睡眠规律被漫长的黑夜打乱时，这些症状就可能出现。{{37}}比如说，一些学生在上学路上睡着，这并不罕见。","sort_order":3}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P6","source_key":"P6-p3"},"data":{"section_code":"P6","source_key":"P6-p3","role":"paragraph","text":"But last month Dragonskolan school installed 140 full-spectrum lamps in several classrooms. Now, stepping through the door in the morning is like walking into bright sunshine. Students even find themselves glancing out of the window, surprised to see that the sun hasn''t come up yet. {{38}}","translation":"但上个月，Dragonskolan 学校在几间教室里安装了140盏全光谱灯。如今，早晨走进教室的门，就像走进明媚的阳光里。学生们甚至会不由自主地望向窗外，惊讶地发现太阳其实还没升起来。{{38}}","sort_order":4}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P6","source_key":"P6-p4"},"data":{"section_code":"P6","source_key":"P6-p4","role":"paragraph","text":"The school is the first to use this simple technique to improve students'' performance. Light tells the brain to halt production of melatonin – the hormone that makes you sleepy. {{39}} Researcher Dr Mariana Figueiro believes that, during the winter, the effects of the lack of light can slowly build up and make your ''body clock'' confused. Exposure to light of the correct wavelength and intensity helps the body to know when to switch off in the evening. So you sleep more and feel better the next morning.","translation":"这所学校是第一个用这种简单方法尝试提升学生表现的。光照会告诉大脑停止分泌褪黑素——那种让你昏昏欲睡的激素。{{39}}研究员玛丽安娜·菲格罗博士认为，冬季里光照不足的影响会慢慢累积，打乱你的''生物钟''。接触波长与强度合适的光线，能帮助身体知道晚上何时该''关机''。这样一来，你睡得更好，第二天早上感觉更棒。","sort_order":5}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P6","source_key":"P6-p5"},"data":{"section_code":"P6","source_key":"P6-p5","role":"paragraph","text":"{{40}} When a sleep researcher at Stockholm told a conference of head teachers about the benefits of installing full-spectrum lights, they objected, saying that pupils would become restless and be unable to concentrate on their work. Money was also a concern for them because some schools didn''t have the budget for these lights.","translation":"{{40}}当斯德哥尔摩的一位睡眠研究员在校长会议上介绍安装全光谱灯的好处时，校长们表示反对，说学生反而会躁动不安、无法专心学习。经费也是他们担心的问题，因为一些学校没有预算购置这些灯具。","sort_order":6}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P6","source_key":"P6-p6"},"data":{"section_code":"P6","source_key":"P6-p6","role":"paragraph","text":"But those like Figueiro, who are arguing for change, point to other evidence to support their case. {{41}} Designers may have ignored the significance of this, as giving any consideration to light in their designs seems to have become too low a priority.","translation":"但像菲格罗这样主张变革的人，引用了其他证据来支持自己的观点。{{41}}设计师们可能忽视了这一点的重要意义，因为在建筑设计中考虑采光似乎早已排不上优先级。","sort_order":7}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P6","source_key":"P6-p7"},"data":{"section_code":"P6","source_key":"P6-p7","role":"paragraph","text":"For all these reasons, it''s not surprising that only a few head teachers have experimented with the lighting in their schools. At Dragonskolan, head teacher Stellan Andersson initially understood this reluctance. After all, some studies suggested that although people claimed the brighter lights were having a positive effect on them, there was no measurable evidence to support this. Equally though, there was no evidence that they actually caused any harm. {{42}} So Andersson decided to go ahead with installing them hoping for better academic performance. But, whether this happens or not, the students are certainly enjoying the bright new teaching environment.","translation":"出于所有这些原因，只有少数校长尝试过改造学校照明，这并不奇怪。在 Dragonskolan，校长斯特兰·安德松起初也理解这种顾虑。毕竟，有研究表明：尽管人们声称更亮的灯光带来了积极效果，却没有可测量的证据支持；不过同样地，也没有证据表明它造成了任何伤害。{{42}}于是，安德松决定放手安装，希望能提升学习成绩。但无论成绩是否提升，学生们确实都在享受这个明亮的新教学环境。","sort_order":8}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P7","source_key":"P7-h"},"data":{"section_code":"P7","source_key":"P7-h","role":"heading","text":"Reviews of songs by teenage boy bands","translation":"少年男团歌曲乐评五则","sort_order":1}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P7","source_key":"P7-Ah"},"data":{"section_code":"P7","source_key":"P7-Ah","role":"heading","text":"A · Fran, by The Warts","translation":"A ·《Fran》——The Warts 乐队","sort_order":2}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P7","source_key":"P7-A"},"data":{"section_code":"P7","source_key":"P7-A","role":"paragraph","text":"A significant problem faced by boy bands is the narrow musical range expected of them. On the one hand, there''s pop-rock; on the other, a dull version of rhythm and blues. Anything that differs from those styles sounds wildly creative, which may explain the phenomenal success of The Wart''s hit song Fran in the music charts. The keyboard plays a central role on this track, giving it a sad feel. The lyrics are cleverly written, with each song-line starting with the last word of the previous line. This is more impressive than you might think just reading about it, because each band member takes a line in turn. It''s a showcase for The Warts'' excellent voices, and lead singer Mike Royce is outstanding.","translation":"男团面临的一大难题，是人们对他们音乐风格的刻板预期：一边是流行摇滚，另一边是乏味的节奏布鲁斯。任何跳出这两种风格的东西听起来都极具创意——这或许能解释 The Warts 的热门歌曲《Fran》在音乐排行榜上的惊人成功。键盘在这首歌中扮演核心角色，赋予它一种忧伤的气质。歌词写得十分巧妙：每一行都用上一行的末词开头。这比你在纸面上读起来要有感染力得多，因为每位成员轮流接唱。这首歌是 The Warts 出众唱功的展示台，主唱 Mike Royce 尤为出色。","sort_order":3}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P7","source_key":"P7-Bh"},"data":{"section_code":"P7","source_key":"P7-Bh","role":"heading","text":"B · Hard Shell, by Loud","translation":"B ·《Hard Shell》——Loud 乐队","sort_order":4}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P7","source_key":"P7-B"},"data":{"section_code":"P7","source_key":"P7-B","role":"paragraph","text":"Loud specialised in disco music until three years ago, when a new producer was brought in who gave them a completely different sound. This has resulted in a string of recent hits although their latest song, Hard Shell, is heavy with electronic synthesizers, string instruments and horns. Ned Laing leads on vocals, guitarist Grant Berry manages a creditable solo, and the band''s rhythm section is faultless. But at just two-and-a-half minutes long, it''s over before you know it. And sadly, the song lacks the memorable lyrics of their earlier successes. In the end, it seems to have proved too polished for their younger fans, and too lightweight for the older ones. It hasn''t been a hit, though many in the industry feel it should have been.","translation":"Loud 乐队三年前专攻迪斯科；后来请来一位新制作人，给他们带来了完全不同的声音。此后他们接连推出热单，尽管最新的这首《Hard Shell》充满电子合成器、弦乐和铜管。Ned Laing 领衔主唱，吉他手 Grant Berry 献上了一段可圈可点的独奏，乐队的节奏组也无懈可击。但整首歌只有两分半钟，还没回过神就结束了。可惜的是，它缺少早期热门作品那种令人难忘的歌词。最终，这首歌对年轻歌迷来说似乎''太精致''，对年长歌迷来说又''太轻飘''。它没能成为热单，尽管业内许多人觉得它本应如此。","sort_order":5}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P7","source_key":"P7-Ch"},"data":{"section_code":"P7","source_key":"P7-Ch","role":"heading","text":"C · What I Lost, by Dealt","translation":"C ·《What I Lost》——Dealt 乐队","sort_order":6}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P7","source_key":"P7-C"},"data":{"section_code":"P7","source_key":"P7-C","role":"paragraph","text":"Though Dealt got together to compete with other popular boy bands, their first single, What I Lost, had few of the elements that defined boy band music at the time: none of the sophisticated disco beats that might have been expected. Instead, it''s understated rhythm and blues with a trace of folk, and accented by that least cool of instruments, the accordion. It was a fantastic debut; four years later, it''s still unique, as different from the usual boy-band style as accordions are from electronic synthesizers, though both were used on the song. And although the subject matter, romance, is familiar, there''s a clever twist: What I Lost is set in a courtroom. It was a track that was hard to improve on, and they never did.","translation":"虽然 Dealt 乐队组建的初衷就是与其他人气男团竞争，但他们的首支单曲《What I Lost》几乎没有当时男团音乐的典型元素：预料之中那些复杂的迪斯科节拍一概没有。相反，这是一首低调内敛的节奏布鲁斯，带一丝民谣味，还用上了''最不酷''的乐器——手风琴——来点睛。这是一次惊艳的出道；四年过去了，它依然独一无二，与常规男团风格的距离，就像手风琴之于电子合成器——尽管这两种乐器在歌里都用到了。虽然题材是常见的爱情，却有巧妙的转折：《What I Lost》的故事发生在法庭上。这首歌好到难以超越——而他们后来确实也没能超越它。","sort_order":7}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P7","source_key":"P7-Dh"},"data":{"section_code":"P7","source_key":"P7-Dh","role":"heading","text":"D · No Grace, by the Bowls","translation":"D ·《No Grace》——the Bowls 乐队","sort_order":8}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P7","source_key":"P7-D"},"data":{"section_code":"P7","source_key":"P7-D","role":"paragraph","text":"Boy band The Bowls'' hit song No Grace features Len Blane''s gentle lead vocals and flowing harmonies from some of the other band members. Say what you like about Blane, but there''s no doubt that he has a gift for writing poetic love songs and arranging them to music. No Grace, which he claims to have written in an afternoon, is one of his finest songs. It''s become a classic guilty pleasure for many people who wouldn''t normally admit to liking a soft-pop love song – but why feel guilty? There''s something touching about the lyrics and they deal with themes that everyone will have experienced at some time in their lives.","translation":"男团 the Bowls 的热门歌曲《No Grace》以 Len Blane 温柔的领唱和部分成员流动的和声为特色。对 Blane 你想怎么说都行，但毫无疑问，他有写诗意情歌并为它们编曲的天赋。《No Grace》——他自称一个下午就写完——是他最好的作品之一。对许多平日不肯承认喜欢软绵绵流行情歌的人来说，它成了一种''经典的负罪快感''——可这有什么可愧疚的？歌词动人，而且写的主题是每个人生命中都曾经历过的。","sort_order":9}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P7","source_key":"P7-Eh"},"data":{"section_code":"P7","source_key":"P7-Eh","role":"heading","text":"E · Floss, by Slog","translation":"E ·《Floss》——Slog 乐队","sort_order":10}},{"table_name":"passage_paragraph","row_key":{"section_code":"P7","source_key":"P7-E"},"data":{"section_code":"P7","source_key":"P7-E","role":"paragraph","text":"Released while Slog were at their short-lived peak, their hit song Floss is a splendid example of attitude plus technology. It opens with a drum solo, which gives way to three minutes of keyboard craziness that sounds like something a far more experimental band might have come up with. The three Slog members play conventional guitars and drums, but it''s virtually impossible to detect them over the unusual electronic sounds of the backing track. Then there''s lead singer Mo Aramba''s remarkable vocals – more those of a thirty-year-old, than of a boy of seventeen. Floss is a marvellous track, and an inspiration for boy bands everywhere.","translation":"《Floss》发行于 Slog 乐队短暂的巅峰期，是''态度+技术''的绝佳样本。歌曲以一段鼓独奏开场，随后让位于三分钟的键盘''狂想''，听上去像一支远为实验性的乐队才写得出来的东西。Slog 的三名成员弹的是常规吉他、打的是常规鼓，但在伴奏音轨那些奇特的电子音效之下，几乎完全听不出来。主唱 Mo Aramba 的嗓音同样惊人——更像一个三十岁的人，而不是十七岁的男孩。《Floss》是一首了不起的歌，对所有男团都是一种启发。","sort_order":11}},{"table_name":"option_set","row_key":{"section_code":"P1","code":"question-1"},"data":{"section_code":"P1","code":"question-1"}},{"table_name":"option_set","row_key":{"section_code":"P1","code":"question-2"},"data":{"section_code":"P1","code":"question-2"}},{"table_name":"option_set","row_key":{"section_code":"P1","code":"question-3"},"data":{"section_code":"P1","code":"question-3"}},{"table_name":"option_set","row_key":{"section_code":"P1","code":"question-4"},"data":{"section_code":"P1","code":"question-4"}},{"table_name":"option_set","row_key":{"section_code":"P1","code":"question-5"},"data":{"section_code":"P1","code":"question-5"}},{"table_name":"option_set","row_key":{"section_code":"P1","code":"question-6"},"data":{"section_code":"P1","code":"question-6"}},{"table_name":"option_set","row_key":{"section_code":"P1","code":"question-7"},"data":{"section_code":"P1","code":"question-7"}},{"table_name":"option_set","row_key":{"section_code":"P1","code":"question-8"},"data":{"section_code":"P1","code":"question-8"}},{"table_name":"option_set","row_key":{"section_code":"P5","code":"question-31"},"data":{"section_code":"P5","code":"question-31"}},{"table_name":"option_set","row_key":{"section_code":"P5","code":"question-32"},"data":{"section_code":"P5","code":"question-32"}},{"table_name":"option_set","row_key":{"section_code":"P5","code":"question-33"},"data":{"section_code":"P5","code":"question-33"}},{"table_name":"option_set","row_key":{"section_code":"P5","code":"question-34"},"data":{"section_code":"P5","code":"question-34"}},{"table_name":"option_set","row_key":{"section_code":"P5","code":"question-35"},"data":{"section_code":"P5","code":"question-35"}},{"table_name":"option_set","row_key":{"section_code":"P5","code":"question-36"},"data":{"section_code":"P5","code":"question-36"}},{"table_name":"option_set","row_key":{"section_code":"P6","code":"shared"},"data":{"section_code":"P6","code":"shared"}},{"table_name":"option_set","row_key":{"section_code":"P7","code":"shared"},"data":{"section_code":"P7","code":"shared"}},{"table_name":"question_option","row_key":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-1","label":"A"},"data":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-1","label":"A","content":"hold","sort_order":1}},{"table_name":"question_option","row_key":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-1","label":"B"},"data":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-1","label":"B","content":"keep","sort_order":2}},{"table_name":"question_option","row_key":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-1","label":"C"},"data":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-1","label":"C","content":"stand","sort_order":3}},{"table_name":"question_option","row_key":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-1","label":"D"},"data":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-1","label":"D","content":"wait","sort_order":4}},{"table_name":"question_option","row_key":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-2","label":"A"},"data":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-2","label":"A","content":"investigating","sort_order":1}},{"table_name":"question_option","row_key":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-2","label":"B"},"data":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-2","label":"B","content":"conducting","sort_order":2}},{"table_name":"question_option","row_key":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-2","label":"C"},"data":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-2","label":"C","content":"researching","sort_order":3}},{"table_name":"question_option","row_key":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-2","label":"D"},"data":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-2","label":"D","content":"exploring","sort_order":4}},{"table_name":"question_option","row_key":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-3","label":"A"},"data":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-3","label":"A","content":"arrived","sort_order":1}},{"table_name":"question_option","row_key":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-3","label":"B"},"data":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-3","label":"B","content":"reached","sort_order":2}},{"table_name":"question_option","row_key":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-3","label":"C"},"data":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-3","label":"C","content":"drew","sort_order":3}},{"table_name":"question_option","row_key":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-3","label":"D"},"data":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-3","label":"D","content":"came","sort_order":4}},{"table_name":"question_option","row_key":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-4","label":"A"},"data":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-4","label":"A","content":"secret","sort_order":1}},{"table_name":"question_option","row_key":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-4","label":"B"},"data":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-4","label":"B","content":"clue","sort_order":2}},{"table_name":"question_option","row_key":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-4","label":"C"},"data":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-4","label":"C","content":"method","sort_order":3}},{"table_name":"question_option","row_key":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-4","label":"D"},"data":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-4","label":"D","content":"formula","sort_order":4}},{"table_name":"question_option","row_key":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-5","label":"A"},"data":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-5","label":"A","content":"again","sort_order":1}},{"table_name":"question_option","row_key":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-5","label":"B"},"data":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-5","label":"B","content":"still","sort_order":2}},{"table_name":"question_option","row_key":{"section_code":"P1","option_set_code":"question-5",